\subsection{F3--F5: Factorial Ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial})}
\label{sec:f3f5_summary}

Trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure are \emph{jointly sufficient in tested configurations} for CIVO. Neither factor alone triggers it: trained embedding without multi-rate exposure (008a, peak NVE ${\leq}0.93\times$) and multi-rate exposure without embedding (008f, peak NVE ${\leq}1.69\times$$^f$) both remain below $\tau{=}2.0\times$. Freezing the embedding eliminates CIVO across 10 seeds (6 Walker, 4 Cheetah; all NVE $< 2.0\times$; Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}), confirmed by $2{\times}2$ verification (exp\_084--087, NVE $< 0.53\times$). Overall, trained $\dt$-embedding triggers CIVO in 5/7 multi-joint locomotion seeds vs.\ 0/16 frozen or removed controls ($p < 0.001$). Partial gradient blocking (exp\_044) does not prevent onset (NVE $4.45\times$) but permits delayed self-correction. Degradation scales monotonically with $\dt$ ($-47\%$ at $\dt{=}2$, $-81\%$ at $\dt{=}4$; Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}). Details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}.

\paragraph{ManiSkill manipulation tasks.}
None of three ManiSkill environments exhibit $\dt$-embedding-dependent CIVO: all value-ratio spikes ($4$--$11\times$) are environment-specific, with frozen controls showing comparable peaks (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}). CIVO is specific to multi-joint locomotion; the frozen-embedding diagnostic correctly identifies these transients as non-CIVO.

